# Agents Guide – OSF Grants Globe

## Objective
- Build and maintain a professional, easy-to-use visualization of OSF/Soros-funded grantees worldwide on an interactive 3D globe (`osf_globe.html`).
- Show locations at city/region level only—never display specific street addresses.
- Prioritize clarity: simple controls, quick load times, and intuitive filtering.

## Data & Privacy
- Use provided CSVs (`2020.csv`–`2023.csv`) and precomputed geo data (`geocodes.json`, `grantees.json`). Run `python process_grantees.py 2020.csv 2021.csv 2022.csv 2023.csv > geocodes.json` when regenerating.
- Keep all processing local; do not call external APIs in the browser experience.
- Strip or aggregate any street-level detail before display. City/region + country is acceptable.
- Cache geocoding locally (`geocode_cache.json`); avoid new external geocode calls unless explicitly approved.

## Visualization Requirements
- 3D globe using Globe.gl + Three.js (`globe.gl.min.js`, `three.min.js`), bundled locally to avoid CDN issues.
- Bubbles/points represent grants:
  - Color by primary sector; keep a clear, limited palette.
  - Size toggles between total amount and grant count.
  - Tooltip/panel shows recipient, year, sector, amount, location (non-street), and purpose/notes.
- Keep performance tight: load in seconds; no runtime geocoding.

## Interaction & UX
- Controls live in the sidebar (toggle button in the viewport):
  - Year slider filters grants up to the selected year.
  - Radio toggle for size mode (amount vs. count).
  - Sector filters with checkboxes and swatches; allow multi-select.
- Ensure the globe remains visible; controls should not permanently cover it.
- Responsive layout: desktop and mobile friendly. Sidebar can slide or become a bottom sheet on small screens.
- Accessibility: keyboard-friendly focus order, high-contrast colors, legible type.

## Setup & Run
- Serve files over HTTP locally (e.g., `python -m http.server 8000`) from the project directory; avoid `file://` to prevent blocked asset loads.
- Required files in the same directory: `osf_globe.html`, `three.min.js`, `globe.gl.min.js`, `papaparse.min.js`, `geocodes.json`, `grantees.json` (or CSVs for fallback parsing).
- Open `http://localhost:8000/osf_globe.html` to validate changes.

## Quality Checks Before Handoff
- Verify data loads without console errors; confirm bubbles render and respond to sector filters, size mode, and year slider.
- Click multiple bubbles to confirm detail panel content and absence of street-level exposure.
- Test both amount and count sizing for sensible relative scales.
- Confirm performance is acceptable on first load and after filter changes.

## Change Notes
- Keep styles minimal and purposeful; avoid visual clutter.
- Document any new controls or data assumptions directly in `osf_globe.html` comments if non-obvious.
